Inlining small images with data: URIs

A data: URI embeds the bytes of a file directly in the document instead of pointing at a URL. The format is data:[media-type][;base64],[data]. For a tiny transparent PNG used as a spacer:

.spacer {
  background-image: url("data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==");
}

And for an inline SVG, which is usually smaller unencoded:

.icon {
  background-image: url("data:image/svg+xml;utf8,<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 16 16'><circle cx='8' cy='8' r='7' fill='%23888'/></svg>");
}

Trade-offs: base64 inflates payload size by roughly 33 percent, the bytes cannot be cached separately from the stylesheet, and a changed icon invalidates the whole CSS file. Use data: URIs only for assets under about 2 KB that appear above the fold. Anything larger belongs in a normal request against your CDN, for example https://assets.example.com/icons/check.svg.

Installing a certificate chain on nginx

Your CA sends two things: the leaf certificate and one or more intermediates. nginx wants them concatenated into a single file, leaf first.

-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
MIIB1jCCAX2gAwIBAgIUEXAMPLEEXAMPLEEXAMPLEEXAMPLEwCgYIKoZIzj0EAwIw
QUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFB
QUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFB
QUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUE=
-----END CERTIFICATE-----

cat leaf.pem intermediate.pem > /etc/ssl/certs/fullchain.pem

server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  server_name app.example.com;
  ssl_certificate     /etc/ssl/certs/fullchain.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/privkey.pem;
  ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
}

The certificate above is public by design, served to every client that connects. The private key referenced by ssl_certificate_key is not: keep it at mode 0600, owned by root, and never in the same repository as your application code.

Verify the chain order with: openssl s_client -connect app.example.com:443 -showcerts

Understanding ~/.docker/config.json

After docker login, your credentials land in a plain JSON file:

{
  "auths": {
    "registry.example.com": {
      "auth": "cGxhY2Vob2xkZXI6cGxhY2Vob2xkZXI="
    }
  },
  "credsStore": "osxkeychain"
}

The auth field is base64 of username:password -- in this example it decodes to placeholder:placeholder. It is obfuscation, not protection. Anyone who can read the file can read the password.

Two mitigations, in order of preference:

- Set credsStore so the values move into the OS keychain and the auths entry becomes empty. macOS uses osxkeychain, Windows wincred, Linux pass or secretservice.
- In CI, do not write the file at all. Use a short-lived registry token minted per job, and unset it in a trap so a failed step cannot leave it behind.

A frequent incident cause is copying this file into a Docker image during a build. Add it to .dockerignore and check published layers with a tool that can diff filesystem contents before you push.

Should you inline your font?

A self-hosted webfont costs one extra round trip before text renders. You can remove that round trip by embedding the file in your CSS as base64:

@font-face {
  font-family: "Example Sans";
  src: url(data:font/woff2;base64,d09GMgABAAAAAAQUAAAAAAAACEQAAAPHAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFB) format("woff2");
  font-display: swap;
}

And the same trick works for a favicon so the browser never requests /favicon.ico:

<link rel="icon" href="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==">

Usually you should not inline the font. A subset woff2 for Latin is typically 15 to 30 KB, which becomes 20 to 40 KB base64, and it lands in your render-blocking stylesheet on every navigation instead of being cached independently for a year. Inline only a very small icon font or a single critical weight, and measure Largest Contentful Paint before and after rather than assuming.

## `type Repository`

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | `ID!` | Global node id. |
| `name` | `String!` | Short name, unique per owner. |
| `defaultBranch` | `Ref` | `null` for an empty repository. |
| `issues(first, after, states)` | `IssueConnection!` | Connection; page with `first`/`after`. |
| `diskUsage` | `Int` | Kilobytes. Requires the `repo:stats` scope. |

### Example query

```graphql
query OpenIssues($owner: String!, $name: String!, $cursor: String) {
  repository(owner: $owner, name: $name) {
    issues(first: 50, after: $cursor, states: OPEN) {
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
      nodes { number title createdAt author { login } }
    }
  }
}
```

### Cost

Each request has a point budget of 5000 per hour. A connection costs `ceil(first / 100)` plus the cost of nested connections, multiplied out. Query `rateLimit { cost remaining resetAt }` in the same document to see the charge for that exact query.

## Requesting photo library access

As of iOS 14 there are three outcomes, not two: `.authorized`, `.limited`, and `.denied`. Handle `.limited` explicitly — the picker returns a user-chosen subset and your fetch results will silently be short otherwise.

```swift
PHPhotoLibrary.requestAuthorization(for: .readWrite) { status in
    switch status {
    case .authorized, .limited:
        DispatchQueue.main.async { self.loadAssets() }
    case .denied, .restricted:
        DispatchQueue.main.async { self.showSettingsPrompt() }
    case .notDetermined:
        break
    @unknown default:
        break
    }
}
```

Add `NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription` to Info.plist with a sentence describing the actual use. App Review rejects generic strings like "needs access to photos".

## `xQueueSendToBack()`

```c
BaseType_t xQueueSendToBack( QueueHandle_t xQueue,
                             const void *pvItemToQueue,
                             TickType_t xTicksToWait );
```

Copies an item to the back of a queue. The item is copied by value; the queue owns the copy, so `pvItemToQueue` may be a stack variable.

**Returns** `pdTRUE` on success, `errQUEUE_FULL` if space did not become available within `xTicksToWait`.

**Blocking.** A task blocked here is placed on the queue's send list and moves to Ready as soon as space appears. Passing `portMAX_DELAY` blocks indefinitely — only safe when another task is guaranteed to drain the queue.

**From an ISR, use `xQueueSendToBackFromISR()` instead:**

```c
BaseType_t xHigherPriorityTaskWoken = pdFALSE;
xQueueSendToBackFromISR( xQueue, &sample, &xHigherPriorityTaskWoken );
portYIELD_FROM_ISR( xHigherPriorityTaskWoken );
```

Calling the non-ISR variant from an interrupt corrupts the scheduler state and typically shows up much later as an unrelated hard fault. Enable `configASSERT()` in development builds; it catches this at the call site.

Style guide: error handling

Never swallow an error. An empty catch block is a decision to lose information; if you genuinely do not care, say so in a comment and log at debug level.

Always wrap with context, and add information the caller does not already have. Wrapping an error as "failed to read file" when the underlying message already says which file adds nothing.

Do this:

```
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("loading tenant config for %s: %w", tenantID, err)
}
```

Not this:

```
if err != nil {
    return errors.New("error")
}
```

Never put a user identifier, an email address or a token in an error message. Error strings end up in logs, in tickets and in screenshots. Use an opaque reference and correlate server-side.

Always make the sentinel comparison explicit. Reach for errors.Is and errors.As rather than matching on the message text; a message is a human-facing string and will change.

One exception to all of the above: in main(), an error you cannot handle should be printed and the process should exit non-zero. Do not decorate it further.

