# vite-plugin-legacy-shim

`npm i -D vite-plugin-legacy-shim`

**2.1.0** · MIT · 61,204 weekly downloads · published 6 weeks ago

Emits an additional nomodule bundle for browsers without native ESM, without pulling in a full polyfill set.

```js
// vite.config.js
import legacyShim from "vite-plugin-legacy-shim";

export default {
  plugins: [
    legacyShim({
      targets: ["defaults", "not IE 11"],
      renderModernChunks: true,
    }),
  ],
};
```

**Peer dependencies:** `vite` ^5 || ^6

**Dependencies:** `@babel/core` ^7.24, `browserslist` ^4.23

Known issue: with `build.cssCodeSplit: false` the legacy bundle inlines the stylesheet twice. Tracked in issue #88.

# "No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header" — but I set the header on the server

⬆ 244 · Express 4, browser is Chrome 124

I have:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', 'https://app.example.com');
  next();
});
```

GETs work. POSTs with `Content-Type: application/json` fail with the CORS error.

## Accepted answer ⬆ 411 ✓

Your POST is a *preflighted* request. A JSON content type is not in the CORS "simple" set, so the browser sends an `OPTIONS` request first, and your app never answers it — Express falls through to the 404 handler, which doesn't carry your headers.

The preflight response needs three things your middleware doesn't send:

```js
res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET,POST,PUT,DELETE');
res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Content-Type,Authorization');
if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') return res.sendStatus(204);
```

Honestly though, just use the `cors` package rather than hand-rolling it:

```js
import cors from 'cors';
app.use(cors({ origin: 'https://app.example.com', credentials: true }));
```

It handles the OPTIONS short-circuit, `Vary: Origin` (important if anything caches your responses), and the credentials rules.

**On credentials:** if you send cookies, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` is rejected by the browser — you must echo a specific origin *and* set `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. Echo it from an allowlist you control, not blindly from the request's `Origin` header; blind echo plus credentials is effectively no origin restriction at all.

**Edit:** the error message is emitted by the browser, so you will never see it in your server logs. Check the Network tab and look for the OPTIONS row above the failing POST — its status tells you immediately whether the preflight was answered.

# How do I type a function that picks keys of one type from an object?

⬆ 143

I want `pickByType(obj, Number)` to return only the numeric properties, typed correctly. My attempt returns `Partial<T>` which loses the narrowing.

## Accepted answer ⬆ 231 ✓

You need a mapped type with a key remap that filters on the value type:

```ts
type KeysOfType<T, V> = {
  [K in keyof T]-?: T[K] extends V ? K : never
}[keyof T];

type PickByType<T, V> = Pick<T, KeysOfType<T, V>>;
```

The trick in `KeysOfType` is producing `K | never` per property and then indexing with `[keyof T]`, which unions the results — `never` members vanish from a union, so only matching keys survive.

Usage:

```ts
interface Row { id: number; name: string; score: number; ok: boolean }
type Numbers = PickByType<Row, number>;  // { id: number; score: number }
```

For the runtime side you can't pass `Number` and have TS infer from it, so take the type as an explicit parameter:

```ts
function pickNumbers<T extends object>(obj: T): PickByType<T, number> {
  return Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(obj).filter(([, v]) => typeof v === "number")
  ) as PickByType<T, number>;
}
```

The `as` is unavoidable — `Object.fromEntries` is typed as `{[k: string]: T}` and there's no way to prove the correspondence to the compiler. Keep the assertion at exactly this one line rather than loosening the signature.

> The `-?` in the mapped type is important if any properties are optional — without it, `T[K]` includes `undefined` and never extends `number`. — *@types-guy*

# "cannot borrow `self.items` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable"

⬆ 165

```rust
for item in &self.items {
    if item.stale() {
        self.items.retain(|i| i.id != item.id);
    }
}
```

```
error[E0502]: cannot borrow `self.items` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable
  --> src/store.rs:48:13
   |
46 |         for item in &self.items {
   |                     -----------
   |                     immutable borrow occurs here
48 |             self.items.retain(|i| i.id != item.id);
   |             ^^^^^^^^^^ mutable borrow occurs here
```

## Accepted answer ⬆ 244 ✓

The compiler is protecting you from a real bug, not being fussy: mutating a `Vec` while iterating it can reallocate the backing buffer and invalidate the iterator. C++ lets you do this and it's undefined behaviour.

For this specific case you don't need the loop at all — `retain` already iterates:

```rust
self.items.retain(|i| !i.stale());
```

One pass, no borrow conflict, and it's O(n) instead of the O(n²) your version would have been.

If the predicate genuinely needs information gathered in a first pass, do it in two phases:

```rust
let doomed: HashSet<Id> = self.items.iter()
    .filter(|i| i.stale())
    .map(|i| i.id)
    .collect();
self.items.retain(|i| !doomed.contains(&i.id));
```

The immutable borrow ends when `collect()` finishes, so the mutable borrow on the next line is fine.

And if you need to *consume* the removed items, `Vec::extract_if` (stable since 1.87) gives you both halves in one pass. Before that stabilized, `drain(..).partition()` was the usual workaround.

Configure nginx as a Reverse Proxy With TLS and Compression

Prerequisites
- nginx 1.24 or newer installed
- A DNS record pointing at your server
- An application listening on 127.0.0.1:8080

Step 1 — Create a site configuration

    sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.example.com

Step 2 — The server block

    server {
        listen 443 ssl http2;
        server_name app.example.com;

        ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/fullchain.pem;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/privkey.pem;
        ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
            proxy_http_version 1.1;
            proxy_set_header Host              $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
            proxy_set_header Upgrade           $http_upgrade;
            proxy_set_header Connection        "upgrade";
        }
    }

The Upgrade and Connection headers are what make WebSocket connections work through the proxy. Omit them and your sockets will connect and immediately close.

Step 3 — Redirect plain HTTP

    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name app.example.com;
        return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }

Step 4 — Enable compression

In /etc/nginx/nginx.conf inside the http block:

    gzip on;
    gzip_types text/plain text/css application/json application/javascript;
    gzip_min_length 1024;

Do not compress responses under about a kilobyte; the header overhead exceeds the saving.

Step 5 — Enable the site and reload

    sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.example.com /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
    sudo nginx -t
    sudo systemctl reload nginx

Always run nginx -t before reloading. A reload with a broken config leaves the old config running, but a restart with a broken config leaves you with no nginx at all.

Troubleshooting

502 Bad Gateway. Your upstream is not listening where you said. Check with ss -ltnp | grep 8080.

504 Gateway Timeout on long requests. Raise proxy_read_timeout; the default is 60 seconds.

The app generates redirect URLs with http:// instead of https://. Your framework is not reading X-Forwarded-Proto. Configure it to trust the proxy.

Client IP shows as 127.0.0.1 in application logs. Same cause — the app is not reading X-Forwarded-For.

Build Your First GraphQL Server

Prerequisites: Node.js 20+, npm, and a terminal.

Step 1 — Initialize

    mkdir graphql-demo && cd graphql-demo
    npm init -y
    npm install graphql @apollo/server

Step 2 — Define a schema

Create src/schema.js:

    export const typeDefs = `#graphql
      type Book {
        id: ID!
        title: String!
        author: Author!
      }
      type Author {
        id: ID!
        name: String!
        books: [Book!]!
      }
      type Query {
        books: [Book!]!
        book(id: ID!): Book
      }
    `;

The exclamation marks mean non-null. Be deliberate about them: making a field non-null is a breaking change to undo later, while making it nullable is not.

Step 3 — Write resolvers

    const books = [
      { id: '1', title: 'The Pragmatic Ledger', authorId: 'a1' },
      { id: '2', title: 'Distributed Enough', authorId: 'a1' },
    ];
    const authors = [{ id: 'a1', name: 'R. Nakamura' }];

    export const resolvers = {
      Query: {
        books: () => books,
        book: (_, { id }) => books.find(b => b.id === id),
      },
      Book: {
        author: (book) => authors.find(a => a.id === book.authorId),
      },
    };

Step 4 — Start the server

Create src/index.js:

    import { ApolloServer } from '@apollo/server';
    import { startStandaloneServer } from '@apollo/server/standalone';
    import { typeDefs } from './schema.js';
    import { resolvers } from './resolvers.js';

    const server = new ApolloServer({ typeDefs, resolvers });
    const { url } = await startStandaloneServer(server, {
      listen: { port: Number(process.env.PORT) || 4000 },
    });
    console.log(`Ready at ${url}`);

Add "type": "module" to package.json, then:

    node src/index.js

Step 5 — Query it

    curl -sS http://localhost:4000/ \
      -H 'content-type: application/json' \
      -d '{"query":"{ books { title author { name } } }"}'

Troubleshooting

SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module. You forgot "type": "module".

A field resolves to null unexpectedly. If the parent object's property name does not match the schema field name, you need an explicit resolver. Apollo's default resolver only does a property lookup.

The N+1 problem: your Book.author resolver runs once per book. With a real database that is one query per row. Introduce a batching data loader before this reaches production.

Instrument a Service With OpenTelemetry Traces and Metrics

Prerequisites
- A running application you can modify
- An OTLP-compatible collector or backend endpoint

Step 1 — Install the SDK and the OTLP exporter

    npm install @opentelemetry/sdk-node @opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node \
                @opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-http

Step 2 — Create the initialization file

Create telemetry.js. This file must be loaded before anything else, because auto-instrumentation works by patching modules at require time.

    import { NodeSDK } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-node';
    import { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } from '@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node';
    import { OTLPTraceExporter } from '@opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-http';

    const sdk = new NodeSDK({
      traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter({
        url: process.env.OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT + '/v1/traces',
      }),
      instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
    });
    sdk.start();

Step 3 — Set the environment variables

    export OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=checkout-api
    export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://collector.example.internal:4318
    export OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES="deployment.environment=staging,service.version=1.4.2"

OTEL_SERVICE_NAME is the one that matters most; without it every trace lands under "unknown_service" and your backend is useless.

Step 4 — Load it first

    node --import ./telemetry.js src/server.js

Step 5 — Add a manual span where it matters

Auto-instrumentation covers HTTP and database calls. Add manual spans around business operations:

    import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
    const tracer = trace.getTracer('checkout');

    async function applyDiscount(cart) {
      return tracer.startActiveSpan('applyDiscount', async (span) => {
        span.setAttribute('cart.item_count', cart.items.length);
        try {
          return await computeDiscount(cart);
        } finally {
          span.end();
        }
      });
    }

End the span in a finally block. A span that is never ended is never exported and produces a trace with a mysterious gap.

Step 6 — Configure sampling before you turn this on in production

    export OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER=parentbased_traceidratio
    export OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG=0.05

Full sampling on a busy service will cost more in ingestion than the observability is worth. Start at 5% and use tail sampling in the collector for errors.

Troubleshooting

No traces appear. Check the endpoint scheme and port; the HTTP exporter wants 4318, gRPC wants 4317, and mixing them fails silently.

Traces appear but are not connected across services. Your HTTP client is not propagating the traceparent header. Auto-instrumentation handles this for standard clients only.

Attribute values are missing. Attributes set after span.end() are dropped.

Add a Redis Cache in Front of an Expensive Query

Prerequisites: a running Redis 7 instance and a service with a slow read path you have already measured.

Step 1 — Confirm the query is worth caching

Before writing any cache code, check the read-to-write ratio and the latency. Caching a query that runs twice an hour buys nothing and adds a failure mode. We only cache endpoints above roughly 50 requests per second with a read-to-write ratio over 20:1.

Step 2 — Set the connection details

    export REDIS_URL="redis://cache.internal.example:6379/0"

Step 3 — Choose a key scheme and write it down

    <app>:<entity>:<version>:<identifier>

For example: shop:product:v2:81ac93. The version segment is what lets you invalidate an entire class of cached values by bumping a constant, without a scan or a flush.

Step 4 — Implement read-through

    async function getProduct(id) {
      const key = `shop:product:v2:${id}`;
      const hit = await redis.get(key);
      if (hit !== null) return JSON.parse(hit);

      const row = await db.fetchProduct(id);
      if (row) {
        await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(row), { EX: 300, NX: true });
      }
      return row;
    }

Always set an expiry. A cache entry with no TTL is a memory leak with extra steps.

Step 5 — Invalidate on write

    async function updateProduct(id, patch) {
      const row = await db.updateProduct(id, patch);
      await redis.del(`shop:product:v2:${id}`);
      return row;
    }

Delete rather than overwrite. Overwriting from the writer races with a concurrent read-through and can leave a stale value permanently.

Step 6 — Fail open

Wrap every cache call so that a Redis outage degrades latency rather than causing an outage:

    try { hit = await redis.get(key); } catch { hit = null; }

Step 7 — Measure the hit rate

Emit a counter for hits and misses. A hit rate below about 80% usually means the TTL is too short or the key is too specific.

Troubleshooting

Hit rate near zero. Your key includes something that varies per request, such as a timestamp or a request id. Log a sample of generated keys.

Stale data after a write. Some other code path writes to the database without going through the invalidating function.

A thundering herd when a hot key expires. Add a small random jitter to the TTL, and consider a short lock so only one request recomputes.

Memory grows without bound. Check that maxmemory and an eviction policy are configured on the server; the default policy returns errors on write instead of evicting.

Write Your First Reusable Terraform Module

Prerequisites: Terraform 1.9+, credentials configured for your provider, and an existing configuration you want to factor into a module.

Step 1 — Create the module layout

    mkdir -p modules/static-site
    cd modules/static-site
    touch main.tf variables.tf outputs.tf README.md

Three files is the convention and it is worth following. Someone reading your module should find inputs in variables.tf without grepping.

Step 2 — Declare inputs with types and descriptions

In variables.tf:

    variable "bucket_name" {
      description = "Globally unique bucket name for the site content"
      type        = string
    }

    variable "index_document" {
      description = "Object key served for directory requests"
      type        = string
      default     = "index.html"
    }

    variable "tags" {
      description = "Tags applied to every created resource"
      type        = map(string)
      default     = {}
    }

Give every variable a type. An untyped variable accepts anything and fails deep inside a resource with an unhelpful message.

Step 3 — Do not configure a provider inside the module

Modules must not contain provider blocks. The root module owns provider configuration; a module that declares its own cannot be used twice with different regions. Declare requirements instead:

    terraform {
      required_version = ">= 1.9"
      required_providers {
        aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws", version = "~> 5.0" }
      }
    }

Step 4 — Expose outputs

    output "website_endpoint" {
      description = "Public URL of the hosted site"
      value       = aws_s3_bucket_website_configuration.this.website_endpoint
    }

Step 5 — Use it from the root module

    module "marketing_site" {
      source      = "./modules/static-site"
      bucket_name = "marketing-site.example.com"
      tags        = { team = "growth", env = "prod" }
    }

Step 6 — Validate and plan

    terraform init
    terraform fmt -recursive
    terraform validate
    terraform plan -out=tfplan

Always plan to a file and apply that file. Applying without a saved plan means the thing you reviewed and the thing that runs are two separate evaluations.

Troubleshooting

"Module not installed" after adding a module block. Run terraform init again; adding a module requires re-initialization.

Every plan wants to recreate a resource. Something in the resource's identity is being recomputed — commonly a name built from a timestamp or a random value without a keeper.

Output is null. You referenced an attribute that is only known after apply, from a context evaluated at plan time.

State lock error after an interrupted run. Confirm nobody else is applying, then force-unlock with the lock id from the error message.
